<h2><strong>Understanding Florida’s Driver’s License Regulations for Snowbirds</strong></h2>
<p>Snowbirds, or individuals who spend a significant part of the year in Florida but maintain their primary residence in another state or country, can use their driver’s license from their home jurisdiction to drive in Florida.</p>
<p>They can also register their cars in Florida using their out-of-state or foreign licenses.</p>
<p>However, there are some key regulations they need to be aware of:</p>
<ol>
<li>If you intend to stay and operate your automobile in Florida for more than 90 days, you are required to register your car with the Florida Department of Motor Vehicles (FL DMV).</li>
<li>If you bring more than one car to Florida for more than 90 days, you are required to register all vehicles in the state.</li>
<li>Even if you are a non-resident, if you drive your vehicle in Florida and it is here for more than 90 days, you’re required to have Florida plates.</li>
<li>The <a href="https://www.flhsmv.gov/new-resident/#:~:text=The%20fee%20for%20an%20initial,charge%20a%20%246.25%20service%20fee."><u>fee for an initial Florida Class E license (if you choose to get a Florida license) is $48</u></a>. To obtain a REAL-ID compliant driver’s license, you’ll need to submit specific documents.</li>
<li>If you don’t drive, you can pay <a href="https://www.flhsmv.gov/new-resident/#:~:text=The%20fee%20for%20an%20ID%20Card%20is%20%2425.00."><u>$25 for a Florida identification card</u></a>.</li>
<li>It’s important to have Florida insurance on each vehicle you want to register.</li>
</ol>
<p>Remember that these are general guidelines and the specifics may vary depending on your situation. Always check with the Florida Department of Highway Safety and Motor Vehicles or a legal professional for the most accurate information.</p>
