<h2><strong>Unpacking Florida’s Insurance Requirements for Snowbirds</strong></h2>
<p>Florida’s insurance laws require snowbirds, or individuals who stay and operate their automobiles in the state for more than 90 days, to have at least $10,000 in Personal Injury Protection (PIP) and $10,000 in Property Damage Liability (PDL) coverage.</p>
<p>PIP, also known as “no-fault” insurance, covers your own medical expenses regardless of who is at fault in an accident. PDL coverage pays for damage you cause to another person’s property in a car accident.</p>
<p>In addition to these requirements, snowbirds are required to register their cars in Florida if they will be in the state for more than 90 days. This applies even if you’re a non-resident and even if you have more than one car in Florida.</p>
<p>It’s important to note that snowbird car insurance is simply car insurance in the state you’re in for that period—in this case, Florida. So if you plan to spend your winter in Florida, you’ll need to ensure your car is insured according to Florida’s regulations.</p>
<p>Please be aware that these are general guidelines, and specifics may vary depending on your situation. Always check with the Florida Department of Highway Safety and Motor Vehicles or a legal professional for the most accurate information.</p>
<p>It’s important to note that Florida does not require drivers to carry liability insurance, which covers damage to another person’s body or property, but it is highly recommended. Liability coverage is typically offered in the form of bodily injury liability (BIL) and property damage liability (PDL). BIL covers medical expenses and lost wages for the other driver and passengers in an accident, while PDL covers damage to another person’s property. </p>
